Definition
A default constructor in Java is a constructor with no parameters.
It is used to initialize objects with default values.
If you do not explicitly define any constructor in your class, Java automatically provides a default constructor.

Q: Simple example of a default constructor in Java
package default_constructor;

class Student{
    String name;
    int age;
    
    // Default constructor (no parameters)
    public Student(){
        // Assigning default values
        name = "Unknown";
        age = 0;
        System.out.println("Default constructor called: Object created with default values.");
    }

 // Method to display student details
    public void display() {
        System.out.println("Name: " + name);
        System.out.println("Age: " + age);
    }
}
public class Default_Constructor {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
       // Creating an object using the default constructor
       Student s1=new Student();
       
       //Displaying default Values
       s1.display();
              
     
    }
}

OUTPUT:
Default constructor called: Object created with default values.
Name: Unknown
Age: 0
